> I've also improved because I have played *all* the classes and races
> in an attempt to understand the utility of common dungeon items. 

This, too, is valuable in avoiding making mistakes.

> daggers. Once I understood how useful common tools could be, I was
> much more resourceful when I made mistakes, as I had many, many more
> methods of escape.

When you have a wand of teleportation and a wand of digging handy, is it
really a mistake to run yourself down to red-level HP next to a monster
you know won't follow you through the hole if you need to bail?  Well,
in one sense it is, but when you're aware of the tools you have
available, you start to realize that it's not so much a "mistake" as a
"strategic risk decision".

The problem is, circumstances that are direct 'don't do this' mistakes
still exist in Nethack, and putting a wand of cancellation in a bag of
holding is still one of those mistakes.

> at least be induced by, mistakes. If you're trying to make the case
> that *winning* nethack means never making mistakes - or worse, that
> this is the central skill for beating nethack, that troubles me. 

No; ascending means making no _critical_ mistakes.  Only a few mistakes
in the game are absolutely critical, but one of those is the wand of
cancellation/bag of holding _when_ all the items you want to keep are in
the bag.  You can literally turn a "won game" into a complete scrabble
for items with that action; thus, it should be high on the list of
Things To Avoid Doing, not Things The Game Needs To Help Me Not Do.

This is the same path as the "warn before danger" patches that have been
floating around for some time followed -- ie. "Do you want to eat the
cockatrice corpse y/n", "do you want to hit the floating eye without
reflection y/n".  At some point, you have to let the player go splat, or
the game begins to follow the Final Fantasy model, where you actually
have to make a visible EFFORT to not win.

I like Final Fantasy.  But it's not Nethack, nor should Nethack become
Final Fantasy.

